.TH IPC_REPLY 2
.SH NAME
ipc_reply, reply_val \- answer a message (SYS_REPLY)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long ipc_reply(long token, msg_t *m);
long reply_val(long token, long v);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipc_reply
answers the call received from
.I token
(the value
.BR ipc_recv (2)
returned): the words
.I m\->w[]
and
.I m\->slen
bytes from
.I m\->sbuf
go back into the caller's
.BR msg_t ,
and the caller wakes. The server does not wait: it goes on at once.
.B reply_val
answers with just
.I v
in
.IR w[0] .
.PP
A server may keep a token and answer later, from any of its threads, as
the console does for a read that waits for a typed line. A token names
the client thread and its call: a call that a signal interrupted, or a
client that ended, makes it stale, and the reply goes nowhere, never into
a later call.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ESRCH
The token is stale: that client is gone, or that call was answered or
interrupted.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.IR m ,
its
.I sbuf
or the client's
.I rbuf
is not readable or writable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ipc_reply_recv (2),
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR ipc_call (2)
